## 8. High availability and the single point of failure

Two Nginx instances do not automatically make a system highly available.

If all clients depend on one non-redundant IP, gateway, DNS path, or
network component, that component can still be a single point of
failure.

High availability must consider: - Load balancer instances - Public or
internal entry point - Network and routing - DNS and failover
behaviour - Configuration distribution - Certificates and secrets -
Backend instances - Data stores and downstream dependencies - Monitoring
and operational response

Common approaches include: - Managed load-balancing services with
built-in redundancy - Active-passive instances with a reliable failover
mechanism - Active-active designs with resilient routing and health
handling

Each has trade-offs in cost, complexity, failover time, and operational
burden.

**Engineering leader perspective:** Ask what failure scenarios the
design actually survives, how quickly it recovers, and how that claim
has been tested. Avoid claiming high availability based only on the
number of instances.

