## 10. Observability: metrics, logs, and traces

### Metrics --- identify trends and symptoms

Useful load-balancer and application metrics include: - Request rate /
throughput - Error rate, especially 5xx responses - Latency
distribution: P50, P95, P99 - Active connections - Backend health and
available backend count - Requests per backend - Upstream connection
failures and timeouts - Retry rate - CPU, memory, network, and
connection-pool utilisation - Queue depth and saturation signals where
available

### Logs --- investigate individual requests

Useful access-log fields include: - Timestamp - Request path or route
(avoid logging secrets or sensitive query values) - HTTP status -
Backend address - Upstream status - Request duration - Upstream response
duration - Request or correlation ID, where supported and safely
propagated

### Traces --- understand cross-service latency

Distributed traces show spans across services and help answer: - Did the
load balancer spend time waiting on the upstream? - Did the API spend
time in the database? - Which downstream call dominated latency? - Was
time spent in queueing, retries, or application work?

### A useful diagnostic principle

A metric tells you *what pattern exists*. Logs help establish *what
happened to a particular request*. Traces help show *where time was
spent across service boundaries*.

No single signal should be treated as definitive without context.

