## 12. Nginx observability lab

This practical lab uses Nginx as a local HTTP load balancer and three
simple backends.

### Architecture

``` text
Client
  |
  v
Nginx :8080
  |
  +----> Backend A :5001
  +----> Backend B :5002
  +----> Backend C :5003
```

Example backend addresses are local to the machine. Use your own project
directory and existing process setup.

### Nginx upstream configuration

``` nginx
upstream backend_servers {
    server 127.0.0.1:5001;
    server 127.0.0.1:5002;
    server 127.0.0.1:5003;
}
```

Round robin is the default selection method for this basic upstream
configuration.

### Weighted round robin

``` nginx
upstream backend_servers {
    server 127.0.0.1:5001 weight=1;
    server 127.0.0.1:5002 weight=2;
    server 127.0.0.1:5003 weight=3;
}
```

### Least connections

``` nginx
upstream backend_servers {
    least_conn;
    server 127.0.0.1:5001;
    server 127.0.0.1:5002;
    server 127.0.0.1:5003;
}
```

### Basic HTTP proxy configuration

``` nginx
worker_processes 1;
pid /tmp/loadbalancinglab-nginx.pid;
error_log /tmp/loadbalancinglab-error.log;

events {
    worker_connections 1024;
}

http {
    log_format performance
        '$time_iso8601 '
        'status=$status '
        'request="$request" '
        'request_time=$request_time '
        'upstream=$upstream_addr '
        'upstream_status=$upstream_status '
        'upstream_time=$upstream_response_time '
        'bytes=$body_bytes_sent';

    access_log /tmp/loadbalancinglab-access.log performance;

    upstream backend_servers {
        server 127.0.0.1:5001 max_fails=2 fail_timeout=10s;
        server 127.0.0.1:5002 max_fails=2 fail_timeout=10s;
        server 127.0.0.1:5003 max_fails=2 fail_timeout=10s;
    }

    server {
        listen 8080;
        server_name localhost;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://backend_servers;

            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

            proxy_connect_timeout 2s;
            proxy_read_timeout 10s;
        }
    }
}
```

This is a learning configuration, not a complete production
configuration. Review trusted headers, TLS, security, logging privacy,
resource limits, and operational requirements before production use.

### Validate and run

From the directory containing `nginx.conf`:

``` bash
nginx -t -c "$PWD/nginx.conf"
nginx -c "$PWD/nginx.conf"
```

Send one request:

``` bash
curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8080/
```

Send multiple requests and measure client-observed total time:

``` bash
for i in {1..10}; do
  curl -s -o /dev/null \
    -w 'status=%{http_code} total=%{time_total}s\n' \
    http://127.0.0.1:8080/
done
```

Inspect logs:

``` bash
tail -n 20 /tmp/loadbalancinglab-access.log
tail -n 50 /tmp/loadbalancinglab-error.log
```

If the running Nginx instance uses a different config or PID file, use
the matching settings. Do not assume a reload succeeded unless the
command and logs confirm it.

### Interpret important timing fields

-   `request_time`: time Nginx spends processing the request until the
    access log is written.
-   `upstream_response_time`: time associated with the upstream
    response; retries can result in multiple values.
-   `upstream_addr`: backend address or addresses involved.
-   `upstream_status`: upstream status value or values.

If request time and upstream time are both high, investigate the backend
and its dependencies. If they differ substantially, investigate other
parts of the request path as well. These fields narrow the
investigation; they do not by themselves prove root cause.

