## 14. Production troubleshooting playbook

Scenario: request rate doubles, P95 rises from 200 ms to 1.5 seconds,
502/504 errors increase, and one backend appears to receive much more
traffic.

### Step 1 --- Confirm the impact

Check: - Is the issue global or limited to one route? - When did it
begin? - Did traffic, deployment, configuration, or dependency health
change? - What happened to throughput, P50/P95/P99, and 5xx rate?

### Step 2 --- Inspect load-balancer logs

``` bash
tail -n 100 /tmp/loadbalancinglab-access.log
tail -n 100 /tmp/loadbalancinglab-error.log
```

Look for: - Repeated upstream errors - Timeouts - Uneven backend
selection - Unexpected status codes - Patterns tied to one route or time
window

### Step 3 --- Check backends directly

``` bash
curl -i --max-time 3 http://127.0.0.1:5001/
curl -i --max-time 3 http://127.0.0.1:5002/
curl -i --max-time 3 http://127.0.0.1:5003/
```

Adapt paths to the actual application. Compare latency and status codes.
A direct request is useful, but it does not reproduce all conditions
experienced through the load balancer.

### Step 4 --- Identify the bottleneck

Investigate: - CPU and memory - Active connections - Thread or worker
saturation - Database latency and connection pools - Downstream service
latency - Queue depth - Network errors - Retry rates - Backend
distribution

### Step 5 --- Mitigate according to evidence

Potential actions: - Reduce or shed excess traffic. - Remove a confirmed
unhealthy backend from service. - Add capacity if the bottleneck is
scalable. - Fix a slow query or downstream dependency. - Reduce unsafe
retries. - Adjust timeouts only when the evidence and end-to-end
deadline justify it.

Do not reflexively restart everything or increase timeouts without
understanding the failure mode.

### Step 6 --- Verify and learn

Confirm: - Error rate has fallen. - Latency percentiles have
recovered. - Throughput is stable. - Surviving instances are not
overloaded. - The mitigation did not cause another failure.

After recovery, document root cause, customer impact, detection gap,
remediation, and preventive actions.

### Common HTTP signals

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Signal                              Possible causes to investigate
  ----------------------------------- -----------------------------------
  502 Bad Gateway                     Backend connection failure, invalid
                                      upstream response, proxy/upstream
                                      problem

  504 Gateway Timeout                 Upstream did not respond within the
                                      relevant timeout

  503 Service Unavailable             Service unavailable, overload, or
                                      no usable backend, depending on
                                      configuration

  High P99, normal P50                Tail latency, outliers, queues,
                                      slow dependencies, uneven work

  All backends slow                   Shared bottleneck, database,
                                      network, or load balancer

  One backend slow                    Instance-specific resource or
                                      application problem
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

These are diagnostic clues, not proof. Correlate with logs, metrics,
traces, and recent changes.

